    "description": "DevOps services help organizations connect software development with deployment and ongoing operation. In Iran, documented container-platform offerings provide one example of the infrastructure these services can use, although product descriptions alone do not establish service quality or market adoption.",
    "tags": ["DevOps", "Software Delivery", "Continuous Integration", "Containers", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:23:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:23:08Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "DevOps Services in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand software delivery automation, container platforms, operational responsibilities, and the evidence needed to assess DevOps services in Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "service-purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting development with operation",
        "content": "DevOps services address the work between changing software and running it reliably. A useful starting point is continuous delivery: maintaining software so that a release can be made on demand. DORA, a software-delivery research program, distinguishes this capability from continuous deployment, in which changes proceed automatically into production. A team can improve release readiness while retaining a deliberate decision about when users receive a change. [s1]\n\nFor an organization evaluating a service, this distinction clarifies the intended result. Automating a deployment command is one deliverable; keeping the application deployable as its code, configuration, and dependencies change is a broader responsibility. DORA identifies automated testing, deployment automation, version control, and continuous integration as supporting capabilities. These are general research findings, rather than measurements of Iranian companies. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "delivery-components",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the delivery system contains",
        "content": "GitLab illustrates how delivery automation is organized. Its CI/CD system uses a project configuration to define jobs and stages, such as building and testing software. Runners are the execution agents that carry out those jobs. The configuration describes the work, while runner capacity and execution environments make that work possible. [s2]\n\nThis creates several distinct service responsibilities: maintaining pipeline definitions, providing suitable execution environments, and ensuring that the build and test process produces a usable release. GitLab supports both hosted and self-managed arrangements, but its documentation does not establish which arrangements Iranian organizations actually use or whether a particular hosted service is accessible to a particular customer. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-platform-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented platform example",
        "content": "Arvancloud's Cloud Container product describes deployment from container images and from Kubernetes manifests, the configuration documents used to describe applications and their resources. Its page presents a panel and command-line interface for submitting manifests. This establishes a vendor-described route for running containerized applications within Iran's technology ecosystem; it does not independently verify customer outcomes. [s3]\n\nA container platform also leaves important integration work. Kubernetes documentation explicitly separates running containers from building source code and does not prescribe a complete logging, monitoring, or alerting system. Consequently, access to a cluster should not be treated as evidence that an organization already has an integrated delivery and operations service. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "delivery-relationship",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How delivery components relate",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Versioned application changes\"] -->|\"Trigger configured jobs\"| B[\"CI pipeline\"]\n  B -->|\"Jobs execute on\"| C[\"Runner\"]\n  C -->|\"Builds and tests\"| D[\"Release artifact\"]\n  D -->|\"Deployment uses artifact\"| E[\"Container platform\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This is a simplified technical pattern, not a map of a particular Iranian deployment. The arrows describe execution and artifact flow; they do not assign contractual responsibility.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "operating-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connectivity and the limits of the evidence",
        "content": "Iran's operating environment also requires attention to connectivity. OONI and collaborating researchers documented mobile-network outages and interference with several services and encrypted DNS during September 2022. These historical measurements show why external connectivity belongs in an operational assessment; they do not establish the availability of a particular software registry or repository in October 2026. [s5]\n\nThe evidence supports an explanation of service components and a local platform example. A defensible account of Iran's DevOps services market still requires dated customer cases, explicit support responsibilities, and independently assessable operating results.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
