    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Persian language processing develops computational methods for preparing, analyzing, and understanding Persian text. Tools such as Hazm, models such as ParsBERT, and evaluation resources such as ParsiNLU address different stages of that work.",
    "tags": ["Persian", "Natural Language Processing", "Language Models", "Text Analysis", "Evaluation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:23:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:23:08Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Persian Language Processing | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Persian text normalization, linguistic analysis, ParsBERT, and evaluation beyond fluent output."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "preparing-persian",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Preparing text for analysis",
        "content": "Natural language processing, usually shortened to NLP, gives software methods for working with human language. Persian examples range from preparing written text to identifying names or interpreting a passage. These tasks require different tools and different evidence of success. Hazm, a Persian NLP toolkit, demonstrates this variety through normalization, tokenization, stemming, lemmatization, grammatical tagging, and other operations. [s1]\n\nNormalization brings text into a more consistent form. Hazm's examples show changes to spacing, punctuation, and elongated letter sequences. Tokenization separates text into sentences or words; stemming and lemmatization seek more basic word forms. These operations can make later analysis more consistent, but they are processing choices rather than proof that a system understands the text. A useful implementation should retain enough information to inspect what changed. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "models-and-tasks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning representations of Persian",
        "content": "ParsBERT is a monolingual Persian model based on BERT, an architecture that learns contextual representations of text before being adapted to downstream tasks. Its authors reported results for sentiment analysis, text classification, and named-entity recognition. Sentiment analysis concerns evaluative language; classification assigns a text to a category; named-entity recognition identifies references such as names of people or organizations. [s2]\n\nThe paper is an important example of Persian-specific model development. Its reported advantages concern the datasets and comparisons used in that study. They do not establish that the same model is the best choice for every later task, nor that performance on a research dataset transfers unchanged to an organization's documents. The practical question is which task, language register, and evaluation collection resemble the intended use. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "understanding-evaluation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Evaluating understanding",
        "content": "ParsiNLU broadens evaluation beyond individual classification tasks. Its research introduces Persian language-understanding resources that include reading comprehension and textual entailment. Reading comprehension asks a system to answer questions about a passage. Textual entailment examines whether one statement supports another, rather than merely whether the statements share vocabulary. The study compares pretrained language models with human performance. [s3]\n\nThis creates a useful distinction between a processing pipeline that runs successfully and a system that solves the intended language problem. A model can produce an answer while still misunderstanding the passage. Evaluation therefore needs examples with defensible expected answers and a clear account of what each task measures. ParsiNLU provides a documented foundation for such assessment, rather than a universal certificate of Persian understanding. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "workflow",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A task-oriented Persian NLP workflow",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Persian source text\"] -->|\"Apply documented preparation\"| B[\"Normalized text\"]\n  B -->|\"Separate linguistic units\"| C[\"Tokens\"]\n  C -->|\"Apply suitable analysis\"| D[\"Task model\"]\n  D -->|\"Produces\"| E[\"Predictions\"]\n  E -->|\"Compare with reference answers\"| F[\"Task evaluation\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram combines documented text-preparation, modeling, and evaluation concepts. It is an explanatory workflow, not a requirement that every model use Hazm or the same preprocessing sequence.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scope-and-selection",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Choosing evidence that matches the task",
        "content": "For an international reader, the central point is that Persian language processing is a collection of capabilities. Text preparation, grammatical analysis, learned representations, and comprehension evaluation address different problems. Hazm documents processing operations, ParsBERT documents a model and task results, and ParsiNLU documents evaluation resources. Their contributions complement one another without being interchangeable. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nA credible assessment should therefore name the task and explain how success was tested. These resources establish concrete technical foundations for Persian text systems; they do not, by themselves, measure commercial adoption or guarantee coverage of every writing style.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
